  "content": {
    "id": "p27-task46-53-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the domain.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x+1}{x-2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The denominator must be non-zero.\n$$\\begin{align*}x-2&\\ne0\\\\x&\\ne2\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $D=(-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2+2x-3}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,-3)\\cup(-3,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.\n\nFactor the denominator as $(x+3)(x-1)$ and exclude its zeros.",
          "worked": "The denominator must be non-zero.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x-3&\\ne0\\\\(x+3)(x-1)&\\ne0\\\\x&\\ne-3\\text{ and }x\\ne1\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $D=(-\\infty,-3)\\cup(-3,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{4-x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,4]$",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}4-x&\\ge0\\\\4&\\ge x\\\\x&\\le4\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $D=(-\\infty,4]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{2x+6}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=[-3,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+6&\\ge0\\\\2x&\\ge-6\\\\x&\\ge-3\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $D=[-3,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "A real cube root exists for every real input, including negative inputs. Therefore, $D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+1}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2&\\ge0\\\\x^2+1&\\ge1\\end{align*}$$\nThe radicand is always positive, so $D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2-x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,2)$.\n\nThe square root is in the denominator, so require $2-x>0$.",
          "worked": "The square root must be real and the denominator non-zero, so its radicand must be strictly positive.\n$$\\begin{align*}2-x&>0\\\\2&>x\\\\x&<2\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $D=(-\\infty,2)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-task46-53-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt[3]{x+4}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,-4)\\cup(-4,\\infty)$.\n\nThe cube root permits every real radicand; exclude the zero denominator.",
          "worked": "The cube root permits every real radicand, but the denominator must be non-zero.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt[3]{x+4}&\\ne0\\\\x+4&\\ne0\\\\x&\\ne-4\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $D=(-\\infty,-4)\\cup(-4,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
